From 3d9201ce365ab5fe4eae60ce31148ac2d954922e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djrobson5 <256682087+djrobson5@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:22:01 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 19/19] Metal: per-encoder GPU timing through counter sample
 attachments

MetalPassTiming (owned by the renderer, one per frame in flight) is set as the
queue's passTiming. While it is active, every render, compute and blit encoder
the queue's command lists begin gets two counter samples through its own pass
descriptor: start of vertex and end of fragment for render passes, start and
end of the encoder for compute and blit. No encoder is split or added, unlike
writeTimestamp, which on Apple GPUs ends the encoder and waits for all earlier
work. A blit encoder made for timing gets a descriptor of its own (the shared
one stays untouched); a timed compute encoder keeps the concurrent dispatch
type.

Each record keeps the encoder's kind and a tag (render passes: the one the
renderer set, through an optional hook called as the encoder begins; the other
kinds are named after themselves). resolve() reads the used samples once the
frame's command buffers completed, leaves out spans with an error or missing
sample, and converts GPU ticks to host-clock seconds with MetalDevice::
getGpuClock: two sampleTimestamps calls 20 ms apart, each paired with the host
time around the call.

The sample buffer is not cleared and the indices restart at 0 every frame, so a
sample a pass did not write would be read from an older frame. begin() takes a
GPU timestamp (beginTicks); resolve() rejects, and counts, spans with a sample
below it or above the timestamp at resolve time. (Metal does write both samples
for a pass with no draw: a load-and-resolve pass and a clear-only pass, checked
in tools/metal_selftest; the check is a guard.) resume() continues a frame whose
records were not resolved yet, for a slot that is begun again mid-frame.

The sample buffer is made with a real NS::Error (halved from the requested
size until the device accepts it); encoders beyond its capacity are left
untimed and counted.
